 "response": "Getosti's current budget is **$2,095K**.\n\n**How I got there**\n\n1. **Only Finance Office budget records count.** Under the policy, notes from Sales, PMO, Engineering, Facilities and others do not change the budget. Finance Office records that don't change the amount (room moves, cost-center codes, audits, receipts) are ignored too.\n\n2. **I worked out which withdrawals still stand.** A withdrawal only takes effect if it hasn't itself been withdrawn, so I followed each chain from the latest record back.\n   - Chain of withdrawals cancelling each other in turn: #0161 \u2192 #0081 \u2192 #0032 \u2192 #0015 \u2192 #0009 \u2192 #0007 \u2192 #0002. #0161 stands, so #0081 is cancelled, #0032 stands, #0015 is cancelled, #0009 stands, #0007 is cancelled, and update #0002 (+$472K) applies.\n   - Same pattern: #1164 \u2192 #0154 \u2192 #0021 \u2192 #0013 \u2192 #0012 \u2192 #0003. Here #0012 ends up standing, so update #0003 (+$547K) is cancelled.\n   - Simple case: #1363 withdraws #0506, so that \u2212$376K decrease is cancelled.\n   - About 100 budget updates end up cancelled this way.\n\n3. **I applied the remaining updates in order.** Starting from the $1,730K set in #0001, I added each surviving increase and subtracted each surviving decrease. The last surviving change is #1590 (\u2212$25K), which leaves $2,095K.\n\nI checked this by hand rather than with a script, so a slip in such a long chain is possible.\n\nANSWER: $2095K",
